{
  "dom_id": "step-e14402",
  "name": "Example: Integrating Products Containing the Exponential Function",
  "problem": "Calculate  $\\displaystyle{\\int x^2 e^{x^3+1}\\, \\textrm{d}x }.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's introduce the new variable $u = x^3+1.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\dfrac 1 3 \\textrm{d}u = x^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{{x}^{3}+1}\\cdot \\,{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
